## Finding: `crime_00022` — Sync I/O in Hot Path

**Evidence (verbatim from scan):**

> - `` `existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `` `readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function) ``
> - `lines: 12, 13, 15`
> - `swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it`

**Blocking methods:** `existsSync`, `statSync`, `readFileSync` — all three from `node:fs`. Each one parks the event loop on a libuv thread-pool request; under concurrent load the pool saturates and every subsequent call queues behind it, which is exactly the p99 spike pattern.

## Fix

Replace the three sync calls with their async counterparts from `node:fs/promises`. The function becomes `async` but the return type stays the same (wrapped in `Promise`), so callers just need to `await` it — the public signature shape (`(void) → unknown`) is preserved as `(void) → Promise<unknown>`.

```ts
import { access, readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

const ADMIN_DASHBOARD = "http://localhost:3000/admin";
const SCHEMA_PATH = "/Users/andrew/dev/crimes/schema.json";

export async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {
  try {
    await access(SCHEMA_PATH);
  } catch {
    return null;
  }
  const s = await stat(SCHEMA_PATH);
  if (s.size === 0) return null;
  return JSON.parse(await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, "utf8"));
}
```

`existsSync` has no direct async twin — the idiomatic replacement is `access()` wrapped in try/catch (or you can skip the existence check entirely and just catch `ENOENT` from `readFile`, collapsing two syscalls into one). The rest is a straight `Sync` → async+await swap. No caching, no restructuring — just unblocking the event loop.
